WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.180
A patch recording gives a curve of current against tension.

2
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.460
Can you read an area out of it?

3
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:13.473
Lewis and Grandl expressed mouse Piezo1 in a human cell line.

4
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:15.893
They recorded cell-attached patches.

5
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:20.167
In a cell-attached patch, the pipette seals onto an intact cell.

6
00:00:21.007 --> 00:00:23.447
It records the channels in the membrane under its tip.

7
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:27.317
They pressurized the patch and imaged it at the same time.

8
00:00:27.977 --> 00:00:30.377
So the patch radius was measured, not assumed.

9
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.440
For a patch shaped like part of a sphere, pressure becomes tension.

10
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:39.760
Tension equals the radius of curvature times the pressure difference, divided by

11
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:40.633
two.

12
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:42.093
Take an illustrative patch.

13
00:00:42.953 --> 00:00:46.453
A radius of two micrometres, under two thousand pascals.

14
00:00:47.093 --> 00:00:48.713
These are invented round numbers.

15
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:52.053
The tension comes out at two millinewtons per metre.

16
00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:54.207
Now the fit from the paper.

17
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:58.847
A half-activation tension of two point seven millinewtons per metre.

18
00:00:59.687 --> 00:01:01.307
A slope factor of zero point eight.

19
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:06.177
At two millinewtons per metre, the fit predicts about twenty-nine percent of the

20
00:01:06.177 --> 00:01:07.177
plateau current.

21
00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:12.337
This fit also hides an area.

22
00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:15.217
You know how the slope factor relates to area.

23
00:01:16.137 --> 00:01:17.137
Can you find it?

24
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.580
Read it as a two-state switch, and run the fit backwards.

25
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.580
The effective area comes out at about five square nanometres.

26
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.060
Under the same reading, the effective resting preference for closed is about three

27
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.880
point four times thermal energy.

28
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.300
It comes from both fitted numbers, not from an independent measurement.

29
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.640
An underestimated area alone does not tell us whether the true energy is

30
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:50.640
underestimated.

31
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:53.293
An independent route agrees in order.

32
00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:59.493
Calibrated against a bacterial channel, human Piezo1 gave six to twenty square

33
00:01:59.493 --> 00:02:00.493
nanometres.

34
00:02:00.683 --> 00:02:04.363
Both estimates assume each channel is a simple two-state switch.

35
00:02:05.383 --> 00:02:06.443
Hold on to that assumption.

36
00:02:07.117 --> 00:02:13.377
Two cautions. This is a normalized current, for mouse Piezo1 in a human cell line,

37
00:02:13.377 --> 00:02:14.537
at room temperature.

38
00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:17.657
It is not a fraction of neurons doing anything.

39
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:23.787
protocol matters. In a separate protocol, a five-second prepulse lowered the

40
00:02:23.787 --> 00:02:27.627
half-activation tension, from two point two to one point four.

41
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.710
The authors' reading: the prepulse relieves resting tension.

42
00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:36.350
Channels it had inactivated recover, so less added tension is needed.

43
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.640
Do not mix the two protocols.

44
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.320
The shift is the finding: the channel's sensitivity depends on the tension it was

45
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.320
already under.

46
00:02:45.583 --> 00:02:49.223
Read as a two-state switch, the slope factor implies an area.
